theorem a-named-term-is-a-number
  note        M names 2N + 1, which is a number as letters and numerals joined by + and · are, so nothing says M is one.
  let N ∈ ℤ                                                           (H1)
  let M = 2N + 1                                                      (D1)
         reads the odd number with half-index N
  then M − 1 = 2N

1.  M − 1 = 2N
    algebra, from D1

theorem an-odd-square-over-eight
  note        As above, in the quotient of an odd square less one by eight.
  let N ∈ ℤ                                                           (H1)
  let M = 2N + 1                                                      (D1)
         reads the odd number with half-index N
  then (M² − 1)/8 = N·(N + 1)/2

1.  (M² − 1)/8 = N·(N + 1)/2
    algebra, from D1
